ion to keep schools closed  in-person learning until september with  case numbers dropping public health  experts had urged the province to  prioritize getting kids back into the  classroom for the last few weeks of this  year but  premier doug ford says the move is too  risky and says keeping schools closed  will help keep summer reopening plans on  track  i will not and i repeat i will not take  unnecessary risks  with our children right now the fact is  we're fighting a third wave driven by  variants  until we get more students and teachers  vaccinated  outdoor activities are safest right now  in a statement today the children's  health coalition which includes sick  kids hospital said it was deeply  disappointed  ontario has not acted on the broad  consensus for reopening schools and  said children must be a priority in the  pandemic recovery  there is a growing consensus the highly  contagious delta variant the one first  identified in india  is becoming a big problem for ontario  it's still unclear how  widely it's spreading whole genome  sequencing is needed to identify the  cases so  confirmation is slow though peel regions  top doctor says he's seen early data and  the trends  are concerning preliminary analysis from  the science table suggests that in one  month  the delta variant will be the dominant  strain in our region with the rest of  ontario weeks behind  dr lawrence lowe says discussions are  underway about possibly accelerating the  rollout of second doses in regions like  peel to get ahead  of the virus from gas to beef still  ahead how the latest cyber attack on a  private company impacts  canada  there has been another crippling cyber  attack on critical infrastructure in the  u.s  the target this time was jbs the world's  largest meat producer  it has plants in canada the us and  australia  as jackson proskow reports it appears  russian criminals  are the culprits more than a dozen meat  processing plants in the u.s and  canada are slowly coming back online  including this plant in brooks alberta  that supplies one quarter of canada's  beef  after a ransomware attack on jbs the  world's largest meat producer  brought production to a standstill this  attack  is a reminder about the importance to  private sector entities of hardening  their cyber security the attack is  believed to have been launched by a  group of russian hackers  similar to the one that caused the  shutdown of a major u.s pipeline  in that case colonial pipeline paid  nearly 5  million dollars in ransom while  americans paid the price  in major disruptions and gas shortages  russia  has basically figured out how to  commercialize cyber criminal activity  the u.s has faced the brunt of recent  russian cyber attacks  experts say the government of vladimir  putin is at the very least complicit  anything that happens on a given  country's territory that country  is allowing to continue we should view  as essentially state sanctions  in two weeks u.s president joe biden  will meet face to face with putin  in geneva armed with a long list of what  the u.s considers  malign activities it starts with the  aggressions in eastern ukraine  and the ongoing simmering conflict in  northern georgia  and extends also to the cyber realm  there's little sense  russia will relent this week the  country's foreign minister seemed to  stir the pot  suggesting the insurrectionists who  stormed the u.s capitol building in the  name of former president donald trump  are being unfairly persecuted we're not  taking any options off the table  in terms of how we may respond the biden  administration won't say how it might  address this latest hack  or russia's continued actions but stiff  sanctions remain in place  while the u.s urges private companies to  better prepare for future cyber breaches  jackson proskow global news washington  the journey's the thing not the  destination next the herd of elephants  wandering through southwestern china  [Music]  an environmental disaster is unfolding  off the coast of sri lanka a container  ship carrying tons of chemicals is sunk  after catching fire  it was carrying plastic pellets and  hazardous chemicals including nitric  acid the ship had been burning for  nearly two weeks  the coast is already polluted and  they're now concerned oil leaks  from the ship's fuel tanks could further  damage the coast experts warn this could  be sri lanka's worst environmental  disaster  a herd of 15 wild elephants has traveled  hundreds of kilometers through southwest  china  they have been on the move for months  now and have destroyed more than a  million dollars worth of crops along the  way  why they're walking is a bit of a  mystery as mike derlay explains there's  now an effort to try to alter  their course it's hard enough for a herd  of elephants to hide in the wild  in a city 15 elephants tend to stand out  this particular herd is all the rage in  china right now they left the reserve in  december  and are now just outside the provincial  capital of kunming  500 kilometers from their home a journey  that is now being documented and  scrutinized on local news  like a car chase  everybody wants to know why they're  moving north says this man  one local theory is that the herd's  leader is inexperienced and just got  lost  which ecologists say is unlikely  migration is a pretty  complicated phenomenon a more likely  explanation is environment  officials have said there isn't enough  edible vegetation on the reserve  and elephants eat a lot so far on their  journey they've consumed  56 hectares of crops it's easy to  imagine that you know  deteriorating conditions at home in the  reserve  might have triggered this movement out  to uh  use utilize areas that uh maybe a  century ago  would have been used by elephant herds  uh you know in the past  it's an issue china will have to address  but right now they need to keep the herd  away from the city of 7 million people  sirens didn't scare them off when the  police tried to block the road with  trucks  the herd found another path they're  elephants  they go where they want mike relay  global news toronto  and i feel like they should be able to  that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen tonight's  your canada is a spirit tree  in the bois de spree forest along the  seine river in winnipeg  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  [Music]  bye-bye
 

